<title>CANADA: Central, east Prairies may see wet spring-forecast.</title>
<headline>Central, east Prairies may see wet spring-forecast.</headline>
<dateline>WINNIPEG 1997-03-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Canada's central and eastern Prairies may see a higher-than-average level of precipitation between now and May, an official of the Canadian Institute for Climate Studies told the Canadian Wheat Board's conference.</p>
<p>REGION  SEASON  PREDICTED    PREDICTED    ANOMALY AS PCT OF</p>
<p>		    ANOMALY (mm) PRECIP (mm)  CLIMATOLOGY</p>
<p>Central Mr/Ap/Ma  -14	     66	    -27 pct</p>
<p>Alberta Jn/Jl/Au   0	     214		0 pct</p>
<p>Peace R.Sp/Oc/Nv   11	    100	     13 pct</p>
<p>North   Mr/Ap/Ma   10	     89	     18 pct</p>
<p>Prairie Jn/Jl/Au  -12	    189	     -9 pct</p>
<p>	  Sp/Oc/Nv   -8	     96	    -12 pct</p>
<p>Central Mr/Ap/Ma   37	    132	     39 pct</p>
<p>Prairie Jn/Jl/Au   22	    171	     15 pct</p>
<p>	  Sp/Oc/Nv    2	     71		5 pct</p>
<p>East    Mr/Ap/Ma   22	    143	     27 pct</p>
<p>Prairie Jn/Jl/Au    4	    257		2 pct</p>
<p>	  Sp/Oc/Nv    1	    131		0 pct</p>
<p>N.B.: Precipitation anomaly expressed as percent of climatology provides a relative measure of the size of the precipitation anomaly compared to the 1961-1990 climatological average precipitation for the three-month period used by University of Victoria's CICS.</p>
<p>((Gilbert Le Gras 204 947 3548))</p>
